+Hierarchy profile authority comes from the captured QSF and `inputs.json`, never
+the working directory's QSF. The driver supplies both explicitly and writes
+`fit-hierarchy.json`, binding the selected profile, exact QSF/manifest hashes,
+input-map digest, checker/config/report/log hashes, resource policies, observed
+rows and exit status. Publish that receipt, not an independently reconstructed
+profile or stale console summary. Existing baseline and FPGA320 floors remain
+100000 block bits and 81920 block bits/96 M10Ks respectively.
+
+For report-only calls, the checker recognizes captured inputs beside the fit
+report. It prefers `project/Plex.qsf`; after normal local/remote project cleanup,
+it can read the exact `Plex.qsf` member of `inputs.tar` **without extracting it**,
+checking both the archive hash and QSF hash against the captured manifest.
+Unreadable, mismatched, ambiguous or malformed authority is refused, not
+replaced with a working-tree default. An explicitly requested QSF never falls
+back to another file/archive. Legacy report-only callers with no captured-input
+context retain baseline behavior, labeled `legacy-default`; they cannot publish
+a captured result receipt.
+
+For a new host-only evaluation of an existing retained slot:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/check_quartus_fit_hierarchy.py --fit-rpt "$slot/Plex.fit.rpt" \
+  --map-rpt "$slot/Plex.map.rpt" --log "$slot/compile.log" \
+  --qsf "$slot/project/Plex.qsf" --input-manifest "$slot/inputs.json" \
+  --result-json NEW_OUTPUT/hierarchy.json
+```
+
+The destination must be new; an existing receipt is never overwritten. A
+hierarchy pass is not a timing pass, fitted-database validation, or deployment
+approval.
